#. type: Title #
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Design principles of operator representations {#design-principles-of-"
"operator-representations}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This guide explains the common design principles and core concepts shared "
"across all operator representations in the "
"[`operators`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators#module-qiskit_fermions.operators "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators\") module.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Overview {#overview}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The operator representations provided by this module share several "
"fundamental design principles:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[**Sparse data structure**](#sparse-term-representation): Operators encode "
"only non-identity operations. The internal data layout is generally inspired"
" by sparse matrix data formats, enabling efficient storage and computation "
"for systems with many modes but relatively few significant contributions.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[**Term iteration and reconstruction**](#term-iteration-and-reconstruction):"
" Irrespective of the internal sparse storage, operators provide a consistent"
" iteration interface, so you can inspect, filter, and transform terms "
"without understanding the underlying data structure, then reconstruct new "
"operators from modified terms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[**Mode-based indexing**](#mode-based-indexing): Operators use abstract mode"
" indices to label fermionic degrees of freedom, enabling flexible mapping "
"from physical systems to the operator representation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[**Term grouping and commutation relationships**](#term-grouping): Operators"
" natively support grouping information that associates terms with group "
"indices. This enables optimizations and physical structure preservation "
"without requiring separate data structures. See the [grouping "
"guide](grouping#grouping-explanation) for practical usage.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[**Arithmetic and mathematical operations**](#arithmetic-and-mathematical-"
"operations): All operators implement a consistent set of arithmetic "
"operations (such as addition, multiplication, and composition) and "
"mathematical functions by using the "
"[`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol, enabling uniform code"
" across different operator types.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"[**Operator term ordering and normal forms**](#operator-term-ordering): "
"Mathematically equivalent operators can have very different representations "
"and behavior in quantum algorithms. All operator representations support "
"various normal forms (based on algebra-specific commutation relations) to "
"achieve canonical, predictable, and optimizable operator representations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"sparse-term-representation\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Sparse data structure {#sparse-data-structure}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All operators use a **sparse representation** where only non-identity "
"operations are tracked. Each non-identity operation consists of a "
"**coefficient** (complex number) and a **sequence of actions** on specific "
"modes.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This approach dramatically reduces memory usage and computation time, "
"especially for systems with many modes but relatively few significant "
"contributions. By encoding only non-identity operations, operations focus "
"only on what matters, enabling work with large systems that would be "
"infeasible with dense representations. Additionally, operators naturally "
"scale to any number of modes; an operator acting on modes `{0, 1}` works "
"unchanged in systems with many more modes since unaffected modes are "
"implicitly identity.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Identical terms are preserved separately during arithmetic operations and "
"must be explicitly combined if needed.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Internal storage format {#internal-storage-format}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Internally, operators are stored in arrays inspired by sparse matrix data "
"formats:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Coefficients array**: The complex coefficient for each term\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "**Mode indices array**: The fermionic modes that each action acts upon\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Bullet: '*   '
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"**Boundaries array**: Indices marking where each term’s modes begin and end "
"in the mode array\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Additional arrays might be present depending on the operator type. For example, [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") instances include an **actions array** of booleans that specifies the type of fermionic action acting on the respective mode index. In contrast, the `MajoranaOperator` class does not require this distinction since it encodes that information in the parity of the mode index. See the API documentation for your operator type to understand the full storage format.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The following examples show how these arrays are organized. First is a "
"direct construction using the sparse arrays:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Tabs group=\"code\">\n"
"  <TabItem value=\"python\" label=\"PYTHON\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct operators directly using sparse arrays\n"
"    >>> # First operator: 1.0 * +0 -1\n"
"    >>> op1 = FermionOperator(\n"
"    ...     coeffs=[1.0],\n"
"    ...     actions=[True, False],\n"
"    ...     modes=[0, 1],\n"
"    ...     boundaries=[0, 2],\n"
"    ... )\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Second operator: 1.0 * +2 -3\n"
"    >>> op2 = FermionOperator(\n"
"    ...     coeffs=[1.0],\n"
"    ...     actions=[True, False],\n"
"    ...     modes=[2, 3],\n"
"    ...     boundaries=[0, 2],\n"
"    ... )\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Combine the sparse operators\n"
"    >>> op1 += op2\n"
"    >>> print(format(op1))\n"
"      1.000000e0 +0.000000e0j * (+0 -1)\n"
"      1.000000e0 +0.000000e0j * (+2 -3)\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "  <TabItem value=\"c\" label=\"C\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```c\n"
"    #include <qiskit_fermions.h>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Construct first operator: 1.0 * +0 -1\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff1[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions1[2] = {true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes1[2] = {0, 1};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries1[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff1, actions1, modes1, boundaries1);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Construct second operator: 1.0 * +2 -3\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff2[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions2[2] = {true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes2[2] = {2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries2[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op2 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff2, actions2, modes2, boundaries2);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Combine the sparse operators. There is no in-place add in the C API, so\n"
"    // the sum is a new operator that the caller owns.\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *sum = qf_ferm_op_add(op1, op2);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op1);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op2);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(sum);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ###
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Convenient construction methods {#convenient-construction-methods}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For Python developers, several convenient construction methods are available"
" that abstract away the sparse storage details. These make it easier to "
"build operators without worrying about managing coefficient, mode, and "
"boundary arrays:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct operators using operator algebra notation\n"
"    >>> op1 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(cre(0), ann(1)): 1.0})\n"
"    >>> op2 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(cre(2), ann(3)): 1.0})\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # The result is sparse even when combining them\n"
"    >>> op1 += op2\n"
"    >>> print(format(op1))\n"
"    1.000000e0 +0.000000e0j * (+0 -1)\n"
"    1.000000e0 +0.000000e0j * (+2 -3)\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```c\n"
"    // The C API uses direct array construction; convenience methods are not available.\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  Individual operator implementations might support additional construction methods suited to their specific use case. Check the API documentation for your operator type to see all available construction options.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"id1\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Term iteration and reconstruction {#term-iteration-and-reconstruction}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Operators provide a consistent iteration interface by using "
"[`OperatorTrait.iter_terms()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.iter_terms "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.iter_terms\") irrespective of "
"their internal sparse representation. You can therefore inspect, filter, or "
"transform terms without needing to understand the underlying data structure."
" You can then reconstruct a new operator from the transformed terms by using"
" [`OperatorTrait.from_terms()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.from_terms "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.from_terms\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct an operator with terms of different orders\n"
"    >>> op = FermionOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (): 0.5,  # constant term (order 0)\n"
"    ...     (cre(0), ann(1)): 1.0,  # two-body term (order 2)\n"
"    ...     (cre(0), cre(1), ann(1), ann(0)): 0.25  # four-body term (order 4)\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Filter to keep only terms of order 2\n"
"    >>> order_two_terms = [\n"
"    ...     (term, coeff) for term, coeff in op.iter_terms()\n"
"    ...     if len(term) == 2\n"
"    ... ]\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Reconstruct operator from filtered terms\n"
"    >>> filtered_op = FermionOperator.from_terms(order_two_terms)\n"
"    >>> print(f\"Original operator has {len(op)} terms\")\n"
"    Original operator has 3 terms\n"
"    >>> print(f\"Filtered operator has {len(filtered_op)} term\")\n"
"    Filtered operator has 1 term\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```c\n"
"    // WARNING: Term iteration and filtering are not yet available in the C API.\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"id2\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "Mode-based indexing {#mode-based-indexing}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All operator representations refer to the indices that their terms act upon "
"as **modes**. A mode is simply an index identifying a fermionic degree of "
"freedom in your system. The mapping from physical degrees of freedom (such "
"as spatial orbitals, spin states, or other quantum numbers) to mode indices "
"is left to the user, enabling maximum flexibility.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"This abstraction is also present in the "
"[`qiskit_fermions.circuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit#module-qiskit_fermions.circuit \"qiskit_fermions.circuit\")"
" module, where the "
"[`FermionicCircuit`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/circuit-fermionic-circuit#qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit "
"\"qiskit_fermions.circuit.FermionicCircuit\") operates on a register of "
"fermionic modes. In both the operator and circuit representations, modes "
"provide a consistent, algebra-independent way to specify which degrees of "
"freedom participate in a given operation.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  **Current implementations use spinless modes**: All operator representations currently provided by this module treat modes as spinless fermionic degrees of freedom. This means if your system has both spin-up and spin-down electrons or fermions, you must explicitly map them to distinct modes (for example, modes 0-3 for spin-up of four spatial orbitals, modes 4-7 for spin-down, or any other convention you choose).\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  This design keeps the core representations simple and general while avoiding imposing a specific spin-ordering convention. Utility modules like [`qiskit_fermions.operators.library`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library#module-qiskit_fermions.operators.library \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library\") provide convenience functions (for example, [`FCIDump.from_file()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-library-fci-dump#qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file \"qiskit_fermions.operators.library.FCIDump.from_file\")) that handle such mappings for you when loading electronic structure data.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  As the package evolves, spinful operator representations might be added that natively support spin degrees of freedom in their data model. These will be clearly distinguished from the current spinless implementations and will coexist with them in the module.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"term-grouping\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Term grouping and commutation relationships {#term-grouping-and-commutation-"
"relationships}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Like the coefficients and mode indices, operators can optionally store a "
"**groups array** that associates each term with a group index. By "
"integrating grouping directly into the operator representation as part of "
"the sparse data structure, grouping information naturally travels with the "
"operator through transformations. This enables systematic structure usage, "
"whether from physical properties, algebraic relationships, or problem-"
"specific symmetries. The structured information can then be used in "
"downstream operations like circuit synthesis and decomposition by using "
"methods like "
"[`OperatorTrait.split_out_groups()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.split_out_groups\").\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"For detailed guidance on how to group operator terms in your workflows, see "
"the [grouping](grouping#grouping-explanation) guide.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import MajoranaOperator, gamma\n"
"    >>> op = MajoranaOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (gamma(0, False),): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (gamma(1, False),): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (gamma(2, False), gamma(3, False)): 1.0\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>> # Assign group indices to terms\n"
"    >>> op.groups = [0, 0, 1]\n"
"    >>> # Partition operator by groups\n"
"    >>> grouped_ops = op.split_out_groups()\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Create operator with 3 terms\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[3] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 2, 3};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[4] = {0, 1, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *op = qf_maj_op_new(3, 4, coeffs, modes, boundaries);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Assign grouping information\n"
"    uint32_t groups[3] = {0, 0, 1};\n"
"    qf_maj_op_set_groups(op, groups, 3);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Partition operator by groups\n"
"    QfMajoranaOperator *grouped_ops[2];\n"
"    qf_maj_op_split_out_groups(op, NULL, 0, grouped_ops);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"id3\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Arithmetic and mathematical operations {#arithmetic-and-mathematical-"
"operations}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"All operators implement the "
"[`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol, which provides a "
"unified set of operations across different operator types. This ensures that"
" code written for one operator representation works uniformly with others. "
"See [`qiskit_fermions.protocols`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/protocols#module-qiskit_fermions.protocols "
"\"qiskit_fermions.protocols\") for how this protocol relates to the others "
"this package defines.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The protocol includes arithmetic operations (such as addition, "
"multiplication, and composition), structural operations (term iteration, "
"mode support analysis, relabeling), mathematical functions (normal ordering,"
" simplification, equivalence checking), and more. See the "
"[`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") documentation for a complete "
"reference of all available operations.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    >>> op = FermionOperator.from_dict({\n"
"    ...     (cre(0), ann(1)): 1.0,\n"
"    ...     (cre(1), ann(0)): 1.0\n"
"    ... })\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    >>> print(f\"Operator is Hermitian: {op.is_hermitian(atol=1e-10)}\")\n"
"    Operator is Hermitian: True\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Construct a Hermitian operator: H = +0 -1 + +1 -0\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeffs[2] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions[4] = {true, false, true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes[4] = {0, 1, 1, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries[3] = {0, 2, 4};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op = qf_ferm_op_new(2, 4, coeffs, actions, modes, boundaries);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Check if the operator is Hermitian\n"
"    bool is_hermitian = qf_ferm_op_is_hermitian(op, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Operator is Hermitian: %s\\n\", is_hermitian ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Clean up\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<DoqAdmonition type=\"note\" title=\"Note\">\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  [`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") verifies that $H -"
" H^\\dagger = 0$ by reducing that difference to a normal form and comparing "
"it against [`zero()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.zero\"). Spelled out in terms of "
"the other protocol operations, it performs the following:\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"  The strength of this check depends on the operator type, because it "
"depends on how completely that type’s normal form contracts adjacent "
"generators. A `True` result is always reliable, but for some types a `False`"
" result is conservative: see "
"[`is_hermitian()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-"
"fermions/operators-operator-"
"trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian "
"\"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.is_hermitian\") for details.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "</DoqAdmonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  The example uses `atol=1e-10`. The `atol` (absolute tolerance) parameter specifies a threshold. Coefficients with magnitude smaller than `atol` are treated as zero and discarded. This is essential for numerical stability when comparing operators, since floating-point arithmetic can introduce small rounding errors that would otherwise prevent equivalent operators from being recognized as such.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  While the [`OperatorTrait`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait\") protocol provides a common interface, individual operator implementations might offer additional convenience methods not part of the protocol. For example, [`FermionOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-fermion-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.FermionOperator\") and [`MajoranaOperator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-majorana-operator#qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator \"qiskit_fermions.operators.MajoranaOperator\") provide a `max_rank()` method that reports the length of their longest term. Always consult the API documentation for your operator type to discover all available functionality.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid "<span id=\"operator-term-ordering\" />\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ##
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"Operator term ordering and normal forms {#operator-term-ordering-and-normal-"
"forms}"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"A fundamental challenge in quantum operator algebra is that mathematically "
"equivalent operators can be represented in many different ways, each with "
"different implications for quantum algorithms. The same operator can be "
"written in algebraically equivalent forms (for example, $a^\\dagger b$ can "
"be expressed as $ba^\\dagger + [a^\\dagger,b]$), yet these representations "
"lead to different behavior in circuit synthesis, simplification, and "
"numerical algorithms.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"The operator representations support **normal ordering** operations that "
"transform operators into algebra-specific canonical forms using commutation "
"relations. This enables reliable comparisons (two equivalent operators have "
"identical normal-ordered forms), reveals simplifications (commutation "
"relations cause terms to cancel or combine), and supports algorithms that "
"require specific operator forms for correctness and efficiency.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Important\" type=\"danger\">\n"
"  Different operator representations might implement normal ordering based on different commutation relations appropriate to their algebra. For example, fermionic normal ordering uses anticommutation relations ($\\{c_i, c_j^\\dagger\\} = \\delta_{ij}$), while Majorana normal ordering uses different algebra conventions ($\\{\\gamma_i, \\gamma_j\\} = 2\\delta_{ij}$). Always consult the documentation for your operator type to understand how normal ordering is implemented.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```python\n"
"    >>> from qiskit_fermions.operators import FermionOperator, cre, ann\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    >>> op1 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(ann(0), cre(0)): 1.0})\n"
"    >>> op2 = FermionOperator.from_dict({(): 1.0, (cre(0), ann(0)): -1.0})\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Direct comparison fails due to different forms\n"
"    >>> op1.equiv(op2, atol=1e-10)\n"
"    False\n"
"    >>>\n"
"    >>> # Normal-order both and compare again\n"
"    >>> op1_normal = op1.normal_ordered()\n"
"    >>> op2_normal = op2.normal_ordered()\n"
"    >>> op1_normal.equiv(op2_normal, atol=1e-10)\n"
"    True\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    ```c\n"
"    #include <qiskit_fermions.h>\n"
"    #include <stdbool.h>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Two different representations of the same operator\n"
"    QkComplex64 coeff1[1] = {{1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions1[2] = {false, true};\n"
"    uint32_t modes1[2] = {0, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries1[2] = {0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1 = qf_ferm_op_new(1, 2, coeff1, actions1, modes1, boundaries1);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    QkComplex64 coeff2[2] = {{1.0, 0.0}, {-1.0, 0.0}};\n"
"    bool actions2[2] = {true, false};\n"
"    uint32_t modes2[2] = {0, 0};\n"
"    uint32_t boundaries2[3] = {0, 0, 2};\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op2 = qf_ferm_op_new(2, 2, coeff2, actions2, modes2, boundaries2);\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Direct comparison fails due to different forms\n"
"    bool equiv_before = qf_ferm_op_equiv(op1, op2, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Equivalent before normal ordering: %s\\n\", equiv_before ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Normal-order both and compare again\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op1_normal = qf_ferm_op_normal_ordered(op1, NULL);\n"
"    QfFermionOperator *op2_normal = qf_ferm_op_normal_ordered(op2, NULL);\n"
"    bool equiv_after = qf_ferm_op_equiv(op1_normal, op2_normal, 1e-10);\n"
"    printf(\"Equivalent after normal ordering: %s\\n\", equiv_after ? \"true\" : \"false\");\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"    // Clean up\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op1);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op2);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op1_normal);\n"
"    qf_ferm_op_free(op2_normal);\n"
"    ```\n"
"  </TabItem>\n"
"</Tabs>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, markdown-text, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition title=\"Hint\" type=\"note\">\n"
"  The [`OperatorTrait.normal_ordered()`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit-fermions/operators-operator-trait#qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.normal_ordered \"qiskit_fermions.operators.OperatorTrait.normal_ordered\") protocol method leaves its positional and keyword arguments deliberately unspecified, allowing concrete operator implementations to define adjustable parameters that control the exact canonical form produced. This enables operator-specific optimizations and normal form variants tailored to their algebra or use case. Check the API documentation for your operator type to see what parameters are available.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""
